{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"# Give Cloud the resilience and config-validation coverage DC gained\n\n## Context\n\nEpic e369 hardened the DC adapter against a real instance and produced a tier of coverage Cloud\nnever had. Cloud's tests grew from INCIDENTS THAT HAPPENED; DC's grew from CONFIGURATIONS THAT\nDIFFER. The second kind generalises, and Cloud does not have it.\n\nThis story is the Cloud half of the parity work. It is lower priority than\n[rebar:0fad-59f7-1605-4377] (Cloud's unpaginated whole-project readers) because Cloud is the mature,\nlive-exercised path — but each item below is a real asymmetry, not a symmetry exercise.\n\n## The gaps, with what DC has for comparison\n\n**1. No per-member 429 sweep.** DC's `test_dc_rate_limit_retry_heldout.py` walks EVERY mutating\ntransport member and asserts each fails on the first 429 — because retrying a mutation on an HTTP\nerror is the duplicate-issue class [rebar:21fc-51d7-90ca-4a03] fixed, and an enumeration is what\nstops a later-added mutation inheriting a retry by omission. Cloud has\n`test_retry_httperror.py` (5 tests) and one 429 path in\n`state/test_acli_subprocess_timeout.py:352-368`, neither of which sweeps the member set.\n\n**DC's property does NOT transfer uniformly, and assuming it does is the main risk in this item.**\nCloud has no single choke point; its mutations traverse TWO seams whose 429 behaviour is\nDELIBERATELY OPPOSITE, both verified in the tree:\n\n* **direct REST** (`AcliRestMixin._rest_urlopen_with_retry`) — \"Does NOT retry on\n  `urllib.error.HTTPError` (4xx / 5xx)\" (`adapters/jira/acli_rest.py:50-52`). A 429 arrives as\n  `urllib.error.HTTPError`. Here DC's property holds verbatim: exactly ONE attempt.\n* **ACLI subprocess** (`acli_subprocess._run_acli`) — **already RETRIES a 429**, on purpose, via\n  `_rate_limit_backoff` honouring `Retry-After`; `test_run_acli_429_retries_with_rate_limit_backoff`\n  (`state/test_acli_subprocess_timeout.py:369`) asserts the call SUCCEEDS on retry. A 429 here is an\n  exit code plus `429` in stderr, not an HTTP exception.\n\nSo \"every mutating member fails on the first 429\" is TRUE for the REST seam and FALSE for the\nsubprocess seam. Writing the DC assertion across both would either fail immediately or pressure\nsomeone into deleting an intentional, tested backoff. This story must therefore establish the split\nFIRST and assert a per-seam property — and it must not fold in a behaviour change (see below).\n\n**If a MUTATING member turns out to be retried on a 429 through the subprocess seam, that is\npotentially the [rebar:21fc-51d7-90ca-4a03] duplicate class on the Cloud side — file a linked bug\nwith the evidence; do NOT \"fix\" it inside this test story.** Whether the existing retry is safe for\nmutations (vs. only for reads/searches) is exactly the question the enumeration answers.\n\n**2. No config-validation tier.** DC asserts a non-HTTPS base URL is rejected, that the insecure\noverride warns, that malformed config propagates, and that a missing credential fails loudly rather\nthan going anonymous (`test_jira_dc_config_settings.py`, `test_dc_pat_required_heldout.py` — the\nlatter exists because bug cd78 was exactly a silent fall-back to ANONYMOUS access). Cloud has no\nequivalent. Whether Cloud CAN go anonymous is itself unestablished and worth answering.\n\n**3. The probe is hardcoded and unconfigurable.** `adapters/jira/probe.py:39` pins\n`frozenset({\"Resolved\",\"Done\",\"Cancelled\"})`; DC threads `resolved_statuses` from settings and\ntests it. Cloud's `probe_remote` also re-resolves env itself rather than using the transport's\nconfigured session, so a Cloud deployment whose workflow uses different resolved-status names has\nno way to say so. Establish whether that is a deliberate constraint or an accident; if deliberate,\ndocument it where a reader will look, and if not, file it.\n\n**4. `search_user_by_email` degradation is unasserted on DC** (the mirror-image item, kept here so\nthe pair is not split). `outbound_assignee.py:33-37` resolves the user-search method by NAME STRING\nacross three aliases; DC provides none of them, so `_bootstrap_account_id_via_user_search` returns\n`None` and the path degrades to string matching. That degradation is probably correct — DC has no\naccountId concept — but nothing asserts it, so an accidental rename on the Cloud side would look\nlike the same silence.\n\n## Approach\n\nTake these in the order above; 1 and 2 carry the risk, 3 is an investigation that may produce a\nticket rather than a test, 4 is a one-test pin.\n\n**Port the PROPERTY, not the test — and note the property is PER-SEAM.** Cloud's retry lives in a\ndifferent place from DC's, so a copied test would assert the wrong seam. What must survive the port\nis the ENUMERATION — that every mutating member is named explicitly, because an ellipsis is how the\nnext mutation inherits a retry.\n\n**Step 0 for item 1, before any test is written:** enumerate every mutating member of Cloud's\ntransport and classify each by the seam it actually reaches — ACLI subprocess (`_run_acli`) or direct\nREST (`_rest_urlopen_with_retry` / `_direct_rest_post_raw` / `_direct_rest_put_raw`) — by reading\neach member's body, not by inferring from its name. Record the table on this ticket. A member that\nroutes through BOTH (e.g. a mutation with a REST leg and a subprocess leg) is called out as such.\nThat table decides, per member, which property is asserted and which instrument observes it.\n\n## Scope\n\n- `tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/` — Cloud 429 sweep, Cloud config-validation tests, a DC\n  user-search-degradation pin.\n- an investigation note (or a linked bug) on the Cloud probe's hardcoded resolved statuses.\n- the per-member seam classification table for item 1 (recorded on the ticket, not in code).\n- No production change intended. Items 1 and 3 may each produce a linked BUG — item 1 if a mutating\n  member is retried on a 429, item 3 if the hardcoded resolved-status set is an accident — and in\n  both cases the fix is its own ticket, never folded in here.\n\n## Testing\n\nThe 429 sweep must assert the ATTEMPT COUNT per member, not merely that an exception escaped — an\nimplementation that retried and then re-raised satisfies a raises-only assertion having issued the\nwrite twice. This is the same correction being applied to DC's own sweep in\n[rebar:2944-dd3f-e982-4359]; do not copy that mistake forward.\n\n**The instrument differs per seam, and a single patch point covers only half the members** — a sweep\nthat patches `urllib.request.urlopen` is blind to subprocess mutations, and one that patches\n`acli_subprocess._run_acli` is blind to REST mutations, while either LOOKS complete because every\nmember still raises. So:\n\n* REST-seam members — observe attempts at the urlopen boundary; inject a 429 as\n  `urllib.error.HTTPError`; assert EXACTLY ONE attempt (no retry), matching\n  `acli_rest.py:50-52`.\n* subprocess-seam members — observe attempts at the `_run_acli`/`Popen` boundary; inject a 429 as\n  the exit-code + `429`-in-stderr shape the existing test uses; assert the OBSERVED, INTENDED\n  behaviour (a bounded retry honouring `Retry-After`, per\n  `state/test_acli_subprocess_timeout.py:369`) rather than one attempt — and record whether that\n  retry applies to a MUTATION, which is the bug-filing trigger above.\n\nEach seam's sweep must be MUTATION-CHECKED at its own instrument: make one member issue an extra\nattempt at that boundary and confirm THAT seam's sweep goes red — proving the sweep is not blind to\nthe half it is supposed to cover.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] every MUTATING Cloud transport member is enumerated explicitly — no ellipsis, no sampling —\n      and classified by seam (ACLI subprocess vs direct REST), with the table recorded on this ticket\n- [ ] each REST-seam mutating member is asserted to make EXACTLY ONE attempt under a 429, observed at\n      the urlopen boundary\n- [ ] each subprocess-seam mutating member is asserted against its ACTUAL intended 429 behaviour —\n      the bounded `Retry-After` backoff in `_run_acli` — observed at the `_run_acli` boundary. This\n      criterion is NOT satisfied by asserting one attempt there, because\n      `test_run_acli_429_retries_with_rate_limit_backoff` proves the retry is deliberate\n- [ ] if any MUTATING member is retried on a 429, a linked bug is filed with the evidence and this\n      story changes NO retry behaviour itself\n- [ ] Cloud rejects a non-HTTPS base URL, or the test records that it deliberately does not\n- [ ] Cloud's behaviour on a MISSING credential is asserted: it must fail loudly, or the test pins\n      that it does not and a bug is filed (this is bug cd78's shape on the other adapter)\n- [ ] the Cloud probe's hardcoded resolved-status set is either made configurable, or documented as\n      a deliberate constraint at the definition site — with the decision recorded here\n- [ ] a test pins that DC exposes none of `_USER_SEARCH_METHODS` and that the bootstrap degrades to\n      the string-match path rather than raising\n- [ ] each new test is MUTATION-CHECKED. \"Mutation-checked\" here means a CONCRETE MANUAL mutation,\n      not a mutation-testing tool: for each new test, apply the named perturbation below, run that\n      test module, record it RED with its pass/fail counts, revert, record it GREEN again — both\n      verdicts on this ticket. The named mutations are: for a REST-seam sweep, make one member retry\n      once at the urlopen boundary (the `== 1` attempt assertion must go red); for a subprocess-seam\n      sweep, make `_rate_limit_backoff` return `None` for a 429 so the deliberate backoff disappears;\n      for the non-HTTPS-URL test, feed an `http://` URL and confirm the rejection assertion fires;\n      for the missing-credential test, remove the credential and confirm it fails loudly rather than\n      proceeding anonymously; for the `_USER_SEARCH_METHODS` degradation pin, add one of the three\n      alias names to the DC stub and confirm the pin goes red"}},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1785693983250020001,"uuid":"0403d503-8e35-4682-8dde-870a47534ef0"}